A||N||6||Diamond Joe Quimby||Pick up Coil replacement||||1||
Z||000000||Diamond Joe Quimby||10-10-2000||01:50 PM||||I have an 84 4cyl. I want to replace the pickup coil, but I can not seem to get the old one off. Do you need to pull the distributor to do this? There is a "four pointed star" piece of metal that is attached to the shaft in the distributor and is in the way. Both the instructions and the shop manual say to simply remove the 'c' clamp, but neither mention the star shaped piece I just mentioned. It appears that unless the distributor needs to come out first, I must find a way to pull the piece off the shaft to get the pickup coil out. Any ideas?<BR>Another interesting fact, the insrtuctions that came with the new part show a vacuum advance, and even the original shop manual mentions how to test the pickup coil if you have a vacuum advance. I thought that none of the 84s used a vacuum advance?<P>Thanks,<BR>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-10-2000||06:18 PM||||I believe you must pull the distributor to replace the pickup coil.  I know that's true on the V6, and I imagine the 4 is similar.<P>The distributor doesn't have a vacuum advance.  The ECM does it.  I suppose the instructions have a "generic" picture of a distributor, and that's why it shows a vac. diaphragm on it.    ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ROB MAC||10-11-2000||02:52 PM||||I believe I had to remove the distributor and pull the shaft up and out of the way. DON'T forget to mark it before you pull it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Diamond Joe Quimby||10-11-2000||02:57 PM||||Thanks for the help. I figured I had to pull the distributor, I was just hoping that I was missing something that would make life easier.<P>thanks,<BR>jim||||reg||1
Z||000004||862M4inCA||10-11-2000||04:53 PM||||You in luck, I just replaced the pickup coil and module on my 86 about 2 weeks ago. What you need to do, is make sure your engine is at #1 TDC compression BEFORE removing your distributor. Now, once thats done, remove all the wiring from the distributor, and remove it. It might be a little sticky (mine was). Once you have it out, you will need to find a small metal dowel, or punch, and drive the roll pin holding the drive gear on. This has to be done since the pickup coil is "sandwiched" between thee two "four pointed star" pieces. Once you have the shaft removed from the distributor, remove the hold down clip holding the pickup coil to the distributor, and the plug going to the module. replacement is in the reverse order. It also might be a good idea, while it's all apart to replace the ignition module. Also, before you reassemble the distributor, I'd suggest cleaning the shaft of all the sticky oil deposits left behind and apply a small amount of oil to the inside of the distributor shaft housing. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Diamond Joe Quimby||10-11-2000||05:02 PM||||Thanks for the tips. I just replaced the controle module, and was trying to replace the pickup coil. thanks again, jim||||reg||1
Z||000006||ROB MAC||10-12-2000||12:08 AM||||And use a BRAND NEW O-ring when you re-install.||||reg||1
